geNorm M-value___
Basically, measures the stability of the reference gene
   A low M --> stable gene (consistent expression across conditions -- in my case; samples)
   A high M --> unstable gene (expression fluctuates)
   
geNorm is pretty simple to calculate:
1. For a gene, compute pairwise variation with every other gene
2. Take the average of these variations; this is the M-value
3. Remove hte gene with the highest M-value (least stable)
4. Recalculate M-values with the remaining genes
5. Repeat until only two genes remain

This creates a "ranked list", the remaining two genes would normally be the most stable/
 best reference genes from the candidate list. However, I do not expect that they will be
 the same for both the enriched and unenriched datasets. Instead I want to get this ranked 
 list, then choose the lowest rank for the enriched, that is also low in the unenriched. 
 I will probably choose this by feel; but could probably take the sum of the two ranks as a 
 defensible way to decide; although it's probably perfectly reasonable to just pick since 
 choice of a reference is somewhat arbitrary (kinda but not really, but also kinda)

I believe it's acceptable to use a threshold for acceptable references; from the original
 geNorm paper, they suggest an M < 1.5 is acceptable, and and M < 1.0 is preferred. The 
 M-values I have gotten have been way under this threshold
 
Luckily, M-values are used in qPCR to validate reference genes so there is a BioC package
 that can rank references and calculate M values. I see no issue with using CPM in place
 of cycle threshold values. I decided to check the genes with the lowest 50 CVs in either
 enriched or unenriched, then took the genes that were in both. These I treated as candidates 
 for the reference gene. txn2 appears to slightly beat out akr1a1b when considering 
 both the enriched and unenriched data; will swap to using that if I feel less lazy. 
 I have spent around 5-6 hours working on this today, so might stop here for now. All of the M-vals
